From c0c37db1e67e8e6decf28de73a315ff101c9bffd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vili Kohonen <vili.kohonen@protonmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 09:20:45 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Add recontextualization: sample under one prompt, learn under
 another

Rollouts are generated under `system_prompt` as before; the prompt token block of the
batch is then overwritten with a target prompt before any log-prob is taken, so the
gradient step is the one the model would have received had the target prompt been in
context. Responses, rewards and advantages are untouched.

Enabled with `recontextualize=True` on GRPOConfig. Leaving `recontextualization_prompt`
unset targets the dataset's own prompt, which is the paper's "-> Neutral" arm.

Three things this has to get right, and does:

- The target prompt reaches the driver inside `extra_info`. Every other non-tensor key is
  popped into the generation batch by `_get_gen_batch` and is not guaranteed to come back.
- Prompts are left-padded to a fixed width, so responses never move and `response_mask` is
  unchanged, but `position_ids` are a cumsum over the attention mask and must be rebuilt.
- `_log_rollout_data` runs after the update and decodes `prompts`, so it is overridden to
  dump the prompt the rollout was actually sampled under.

`algorithm.rollout_correction.bypass_mode` would set old_log_probs from the rollout engine's
own log-probs, computed under the sampling prompt, which voids the method with no visible
symptom; it is asserted off.

tests/test_recontextualization.py covers the tensor surgery on CPU with no verl, no GPU and
no model download, including a check that the swapped batch scores the response exactly as an
unpadded forward pass would.
